from a1 import t
C5='Salvation and morality'
t('faith-and-works', C5, 'Faith and works', 'We are saved by faith alone, not by works. Catholics think they earn salvation.',
 ["Catholics do not believe we earn salvation. The Church teaches that justification is God’s free gift of grace, received through faith, which we cannot merit (CCC 1996-1998, 2010). Paul says, “by grace you have been saved through faith; and this is not your own doing, it is the gift of God, not because of works” (Ephesians 2:8-9). But the same passage goes on, “we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works” (2:10), and Paul speaks of “faith working through love” (Galatians 5:6).",
  "James says plainly, “a man is justified by works and not by faith alone” (James 2:24), and “faith apart from works is dead” (2:26). Paul and James are not contradicting each other: Paul rejects the “works of the law” (the Jewish rituals as a way to claim God’s favour), while James rejects a faith that does nothing. Jesus’ account of the last judgement is based on what we did for “the least of these” (Matthew 25:31-46). Once God’s grace makes us just, the good we do is truly ours, and yet also his gift. “Our merit is God’s gift” (St. Augustine, quoted in CCC 2009)."],
 [('Ephesians 2:8-10','By grace you have been saved through faith'),('James 2:14-26','Faith apart from works is dead'),('Galatians 5:6','Faith working through love'),('Romans 2:6-8','He will render to each according to his works'),('Matthew 25:31-46','The judgement of the nations'),('Philippians 2:12-13','Work out your own salvation; it is God who works in you')],
 [1987,1988,1989,1990,1991,1992,1996,1997,1998,2008,2009,2010],['Salvation','Faith','Works','Justification'],['once-saved','baptismal-regeneration'])

t('once-saved', C5, 'Once saved, always saved?', 'Once a person is saved, he can never lose his salvation.',
 ["The Church teaches that we can have real hope and trust in God’s mercy, but not an absolute certainty that we will persevere. The New Testament warns believers that they can fall away. Paul says, “let anyone who thinks that he stands take heed lest he fall” (1 Corinthians 10:12), and fears that “after preaching to others I myself should be disqualified” (1 Corinthians 9:27). Hebrews 6:4-6 and 10:26-31 warn of those who, having received the light, fall away. Jesus describes servants who are cast out for failing to use God’s gifts (Matthew 25:14-30), and branches that are cut off from the vine (John 15:1-6). Paul speaks of salvation as something we are being saved in hope (Romans 8:24), and speaks of a race still to be finished (2 Timothy 4:7-8).",
  "Mortal sin, a grave sin committed with full knowledge and consent, can destroy the life of grace in us, and the sacrament of Confession is the way God restores it. The Church also teaches that God gives all the help we need, and we can trust in him: “Whoever endures to the end will be saved” (Matthew 24:13)."],
 [('1 Corinthians 10:1-12','Let anyone who thinks he stands take heed'),('Hebrews 6:4-8','Those who have fallen away'),('John 15:1-6','Abide in me; branches that do not bear fruit are cut off'),('Philippians 2:12','Work out your salvation with fear and trembling'),('Matthew 24:13','He who endures to the end will be saved')],
 [1021,1033,1036,1037,1855,1856,1857,1861,2016],['Salvation','Perseverance','Mortal sin'],['faith-and-works','confession'])

t('indulgences', C5, 'Indulgences', 'Indulgences are the selling of forgiveness.',
 ["An indulgence does not forgive sins and has never been permission to sin. Forgiveness of sin comes through repentance and the sacraments. An indulgence concerns the “temporal punishment” that may remain after sin is forgiven, much as a person who is forgiven for breaking a window still needs to repair it. The Church can apply to us the spiritual treasury built up by Christ and the saints, since we are all members of one Body (Colossians 1:24; 1 Corinthians 12:26).",
  "The Church has always taught that the sale of indulgences was an abuse, and in 1567 Pope Pius V banned any financial gain from them. The Council of Trent had already reformed the practice. Today, an indulgence is gained by doing some prayer or good work, along with Confession, Communion, and prayer for the pope’s intentions. God’s mercy is always freely given."],
 [('Matthew 16:19','Whatever you loose on earth'),('Colossians 1:24','Completing what is lacking in Christ’s afflictions for the sake of his body'),('2 Samuel 12:13-14','David forgiven, yet the child dies'),('1 Corinthians 3:13-15','Saved, but as through fire')],
 [1471,1472,1473,1474,1475,1476,1477,1478,1479],['Indulgences','Purgatory','Temporal punishment'],['purgatory'])

t('purgatory', C5, 'Purgatory', 'Purgatory is not in the Bible. Christ’s sacrifice is enough.',
 ["Purgatory is the final purification of those who die in God’s grace and friendship but are not yet fully cleansed, so that they may enter the joy of heaven. The Church does not teach that it is a second chance or a place of torment, but that “nothing unclean shall enter” heaven (Revelation 21:27) and that we are made holy by God’s love, which Scripture often compares to fire (1 Corinthians 3:13-15; 1 Peter 1:6-7; Malachi 3:2-3). Paul describes a person whose work is burned yet “he himself will be saved, but only as through fire”.",
  "Praying for the dead is an ancient Jewish and Christian practice (2 Maccabees 12:44-46), and it makes sense only if the dead can still be helped. Jesus speaks of sins forgiven “in the age to come” (Matthew 12:32) and of a prison from which one does not come out “till you have paid the last penny” (Matthew 5:25-26). The word “purgatory” is not in the Bible, but the Church’s tradition, from the early inscriptions of the catacombs to Saint Augustine, includes prayers for the dead. Christ’s sacrifice is fully sufficient; purgatory is about being made able to receive it fully."],
 [('1 Corinthians 3:11-15','Saved, but only as through fire'),('2 Maccabees 12:41-46','Prayer for the dead'),('Matthew 12:31-32','Not forgiven in this age or in the age to come'),('Revelation 21:27','Nothing unclean shall enter'),('1 Peter 4:6','The gospel preached even to the dead')],
 [1030,1031,1032,1472],['Purgatory','Prayer for the dead','Purification'],['indulgences','intercession-of-saints'])

t('hell', C5, 'Hell', 'A loving God would not send anyone to hell.',
 ["The Church teaches that hell is real: it is the state of definitive self-exclusion from communion with God, and God does not predestine anyone to it. Jesus speaks about it more than any other person in the New Testament (Matthew 25:41, 46; Mark 9:43-48). God’s love respects our freedom: C. S. Lewis said that the doors of hell are locked on the inside. A love that forced us to love him back would not be love.",
  "To go to hell, one must die in mortal sin without repenting, having freely chosen to turn away from God. The Church has never said that any particular person is in hell, not even Judas, and prays that all be saved. The existence of hell takes seriously the weight of our choices, and our responsibility, and makes our lives matter."],
 [('Matthew 25:31-46','The sheep and the goats'),('Luke 16:19-31','The rich man and Lazarus'),('2 Peter 3:9','God is patient, not wishing that any should perish'),('1 Timothy 2:3-4','God desires all to be saved'),('Matthew 7:13-14','The narrow gate')],
 [1033,1034,1035,1036,1037,1058],['Hell','Judgement','Free will'],['once-saved'])

t('sunday-sabbath', C5, 'Sunday or Saturday?', 'The Bible says the Sabbath is Saturday; Catholics changed it.',
 ["The Church keeps Sunday as the Lord’s Day because Jesus rose from the dead on “the first day of the week” (Mark 16:2, 9; John 20:1) and appeared to the disciples on the following Sunday (John 20:26). The first Christians gathered “on the first day of the week to break bread” (Acts 20:7) and put aside their offerings that day (1 Corinthians 16:2). John calls it “the Lord’s Day” (Revelation 1:10). The Catechism says the Sunday celebration fulfils the Sabbath and “announces the eternal rest of man in God” (CCC 2175).",
  "Paul says that no one should judge Christians “in questions of food and drink or with regard to a festival or a new moon or a sabbath” (Colossians 2:16-17), and the Council of Jerusalem did not require Gentile believers to keep the Mosaic law (Acts 15). The idea that the Catholic Church “changed” the day in the fourth century is not historically accurate: Sunday worship is attested already in Ignatius and Justin Martyr in the second century. The Church still asks for the rest and the worship that the commandment commands."],
 [('Acts 20:7','On the first day of the week we gathered to break bread'),('John 20:19-26','The risen Lord appears on the first day, and a week later'),('Revelation 1:10','On the Lord’s Day'),('Colossians 2:16-17','Let no one pass judgement on you about a sabbath'),('Exodus 20:8-11','Remember the sabbath day')],
 [2174,2175,2176,2177,2178,2179,2180,2181,2182,2183],['Sunday','Sabbath','Lord’s Day','Third commandment'],[])

t('images', C5, 'Statues and images', 'Catholics worship idols; the Bible forbids carved images.',
 ["The first commandment forbids making idols to worship (Exodus 20:4-5), but God himself commanded images in the Tabernacle and Temple: the carved cherubim on the Ark of the Covenant (Exodus 25:18-20), the bronze serpent (Numbers 21:8-9) and the cherubim and palm trees carved in Solomon’s Temple (1 Kings 6:23-29). The commandment is against worshipping what we have made as a god, not against using images to remind us of God and his friends, as we keep photographs of loved ones.",
  "Since the Word became flesh, God can be depicted: “Whoever has seen me has seen the Father” (John 14:9). The Second Council of Nicaea (787) taught that honour (dulia) given to an image passes to the person it represents, and is very different from the adoration (latria) that belongs to God alone. Catholics do not pray to statues, but to the saints they remind us of."],
 [('Exodus 25:18-22','God commands carved cherubim for the Ark'),('Numbers 21:8-9','The bronze serpent'),('1 Kings 6:23-29','Carved cherubim in the Temple'),('Exodus 20:4-5','You shall not make a graven image to worship it'),('Colossians 1:15','He is the image of the invisible God')],
 [2129,2130,2131,2132,2141,1159,1160,1161,1162],['Images','Icons','Idolatry','Statues'],['intercession-of-saints'])
